.TH FPALD 8 "November 2026" "fpal @VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
fpald \- tells GNOME that a fingerprint sensor exists
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fpald
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR ms ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR socket ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
GNOME's login and lock screens ask the D-Bus service
.B net.reactivated.Fprint
whether a fingerprint reader exists before they offer one.
.B fpald
answers that question: it reports one reader of type
.B press
when a supported sensor is plugged in, and nothing otherwise.
.PP
It never opens the sensor, and it refuses every method that would claim,
verify, enrol or delete. The verification itself runs through the PAM service
.B gdm-fingerprint
and
.BR pam_fpal (8).
.PP
It is started by systemd (\c
.BR fpald.service ),
which
.B authselect\-fpal.sh install
enables. Only one program can own the D-Bus name, so
.B fprintd
is masked at the same time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
Verbose log messages.
.TP
.BI \-t " ms"
Exit after this many milliseconds without traffic (default 0: never).
.TP
.BI \-s " socket"
Connect to this bus socket instead of the system bus.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fpal (8),
.BR pam_fpal (8)
